regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_disposition_20260908.txt:93:[Debugger byte writes](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugger/debugger_cmds.c:1502) print the requested value and call memory access with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`. The [memory layer](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:584) then skips device callbacks unless `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS` is set. The [UART registers with `DM_DEFAULT`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ns16550.c:353), so those debugger writes do not change its device state through `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)`. The skipped path can still return `MEMORY_ACCESS_OK`.
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_disposition_20260908.txt:99:There is also newer evidence now present in the workspace: [WITNESS_HEAD_20260908.out](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/WITNESS_HEAD_20260908.out:1) reports both controls alive, the UART session dead, and the stub diagnostic present—`2/4` because its expectations describe repaired behavior. Those red rows are positive witness evidence on the labelled build. I did not execute that run or establish its exact binary revision. Its [probe also documents a BWX dependency](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py:25), limiting what it says about a hardware-faithful driver sequence.
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_analysis_20260908.txt:23:The LCA endpoint has **two direct upstream connections**: the generic ISA aggregate and the primary 8259 PIC. The secondary PIC connects indirectly through `.lca.isa.2`. The bus registers **sixteen child endpoints**, `.isa.0` through `.isa.15`. Its peripheral wiring supplies **seven unconditional IRQ inputs**: timer 0, keyboard 1, serial 3 and 4, parallel 7, RTC 8 and mouse 12; **two conditional IDE inputs**, 14 and 15, are added when their associated disks exist. Registration does not mean every source starts enabled—the timer, for example, is created with `in_use=0`. [bus_isa.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:190)
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_analysis_20260908.answer.txt:39:  The LCA endpoint has **two direct upstream connections**: the generic ISA aggregate and the primary 8259 PIC. The secondary PIC connects indirectly through `.lca.isa.2`. The bus registers **sixteen child endpoints**, `.isa.0` through `.isa.15`. Its peripheral wiring supplies **seven unconditional IRQ inputs**: timer 0, keyboard 1, serial 3 and 4, parallel 7, RTC 8 and mouse 12; **two conditional IDE inputs**, 14 and 15, are added when their associated disks exist. Registration does not mean every source starts enabled—the timer, for example, is created with `in_use=0`. [bus_isa.c](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:190)
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_reach_20260907.txt:1:**VERDICT: Keyboard, both UARTs, and instantiated IDE controllers have synchronous guest-access assertion paths. PIT/IRQ 0 is present; PIT and RTC assertions require host-timer events. An ISA assertion propagated to LCA reaches a TODO handler that exits the emulator.**
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_reach_20260907.txt:11:Every `B+offset` below is therefore the requested machine-specific mapping—for example, UART0 starts at `0x1d00003f8`. [dev_lca.c:47](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:47), [alpha_lcareg.h:45](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:45)
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_reach_20260907.txt:20:| NS16550 `tty1` | **3** | `B+0x2f8`–`B+0x2ff` | Same UART paths. Its `in_use=0` does **not** disable the interrupt logic: `dev_ns16550_tick` does not test `in_use`. [dev_ns16550.c, tick/access](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ns16550.c:74), [MCR path:282](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ns16550.c:282) |
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_reach_20260907.txt:53:- **Inside a guest load/store:** UART, keyboard command/reply handling, WDC, and PIC recalculation.
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_reach_20260907.txt:54:- **Scheduled device ticks without a host-timer prerequisite:** UART/keyboard input polling and reassertion of existing WDC state.
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_implement_v2_20260908.txt:7:The decisive wiring is immediately after the registration: [`devinit_lca()` passes the same interrupt path to `bus_isa_init()`](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:405). That function [connects its upstream interrupt to the supplied template](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:190) and unconditionally installs the [first UART on ISA IRQ 4](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:250).
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_implement_v2_20260908.txt:11:`dev_ns16550_access()` / scheduled UART tick → `dev_ns16550_tick()` → `isa_interrupt_assert()` → `isa_interrupt_common()` → `lca_interrupt_assert()` → `exit(1)`.
regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill\codex_implement_v2_20260908.txt:13:The [UART’s interrupt logic](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ns16550.c:74) asserts for enabled pending serial events. The [ISA logic](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:52) records the request, applies PIC masks, records `last_int`, and forwards an unmasked request. This requires ordinary guest driver/MMIO operation, with no machine-description modification. It does not establish an unprivileged guest-application trigger.
